<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>3.1.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00702</md:content-id>
    <md:title>3.1.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b65457bf-34b1-4f71-a22e-6b2b13b71a49</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>

        <exercise id="_30068c90-b089-4b92-a495-446e12f918e8" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00002">1. The scatter plot shows the number of times a player came to bat and the number
              of hits they had.</para>
            <para id="para-00003">
              <media alt="A scatter plot. Horizontal, 0 to 600, by 50’s, labeled at bats. Vertical, 0 to 150, by 12 point 5’s, labeled hits. 19 dots trend linearly up and to the right.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_03_13_01.svg"/>
              </media>

            </para>

            <para id="para-00004">The scatter plot includes a point at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>318</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>80</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. Describe the meaning of this
              point in this situation.</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">Correct! For every 318 at bats, there are 80 hits.</para>
            <para id="para-00006">Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00007">The correct answer is that for every 318 at bats, there are 80 hits.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <para id="para-00008">For questions 2—5, use the following to answer each question.</para>
            <para id="para-00009">
              <quote id="quote-00001">The scatter plot shows the number of minutes people had to wait for service at
              a restaurant and the number of waitstaff available at the time.</quote>
              <quote id="quote-00002">A line
              that models the data is given by the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1.62</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents
              the wait time, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the number of waitstaff available.</quote>
            </para>
        <exercise id="c2210f3f-1767-4b78-a5b5-643e917e2f0d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00010">The slope of the line is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1.62</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. What does this mean in this situation?</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00011">Correct! The slope means that for every increase of 1 staff person, the wait time
              decreases 1.62 minutes.</para>
            <para id="para-00012">Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00013">The correct answer is the slope means that for every increase of 1 staff person,
              the wait time decreases 1.62 minutes.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_4d5749f2-bca8-4182-880f-b7cd08b57751" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00014">The slope of the line is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1.62</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. Is it realistic to conclude, for this slope,
              as <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> increases, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> will decrease?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00015">Correct! Yes. For every increase of staff people, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, the wait time, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>,
              decreases. For an increase of 1 staff person, the wait time decreases 1.62 minutes.
              This is realistic.</para>
            <para id="para-00016">Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00017">The correct answer is: Yes. For every increase of staff people, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, the wait
              time, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, decreases. For an increase of 1 staff person, the wait time decreases
              1.62 minutes. This is realistic.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="b1a4bbef-f1a5-4164-a881-9907e963311e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00018">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. What does this mean in this situation?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00019">Correct! When there are 0 staff people, the wait time is 18 minutes. This is not
              realistic when there are no staff available. </para>
            <para id="para-00020">Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00021">The correct answer is when there are 0 staff people, the wait time is 18 minutes.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f696961c-25dc-45ae-9779-26781eddb56d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00022">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. Is this a realistic <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept point if
              <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the wait time and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the number of staff available?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00023">Correct! No, this is not realistic when there are no staff available.</para>
            <para id="para-00024">Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00025">The correct answer is: No, this is not realistic when there are no staff
              available.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_1086b591-c6a7-4c6a-a760-8716500fefc2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00026">The company’s pricing plan states that the usage rate is constant for any number
              of minutes connected to the Internet. The number of minutes is related to the cost in
              dollars and has a linear relationship. The equation of the line is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.03</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>0.10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. What does the slope represent in this situation?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00027">Correct! For every minute of Internet usage, the price increases 3 cents.</para>
            <para id="para-00028">Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00029">The correct answer is for every minute of Internet used, the price increases 3
              cents.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0893a163-5d6c-4fc7-a03a-fd2f1e15aa4d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00030">Below is a scatter plot of the data with two linear models, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>130</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and
              <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>25.3</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3.66</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. Which of these two models does a better job of describing how shoe
              length <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and height <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> (in inches) are related?</para>
            <para id="para-00031">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mn>25.3</m:mn><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mn>3.66</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>130</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00034">Correct! The line of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>25.3</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3.66</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is a better choice because as shoe
              length increases, height tends to increase.</para>
            <para id="para-00035">Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00036">The correct answer is the line of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>25.3</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3.66</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is a better choice because
              as shoe length increases, height tends to increase.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_690d7104-ba71-4fc3-9335-5e53a6db81e2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00037">The scatter plot below displays the elevation and median number of clear days per
              year of 14 U.S. cities. Two lines are shown on the scatter plot. Which line fits the
              data better?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00038">Correct! Line 2 fits the data better because more points seem to be closer to the
              line.</para>
            <para id="para-00039">Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00040">The correct answer is Line 2 fits the data better because more points seem to be
              closer to the line.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e7c962cd-ccb9-477a-8982-6a32387fe740" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00041">According to the scatter plot below, what claim can be made about women’s shoe
              length and height?</para>
            <media alt="A SCATTER PLOT THAT SHOWS SHOE LENGTH IN INCHES ON THE X-AXIS AND HEIGHT IN INCHES ON THE Y-AXIS. ">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_03_13_02.jpg"/>
            </media>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00042">Correct! As a woman’s shoe length increases, her height also tends to increase.</para>
            <para id="para-00043">Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00044">The correct answer is: As a woman’s shoe length increases, her height also tends
              to increase.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <para id="para-00045">For questions 10 – 12, use the scatter plot below.</para>
      <para id="para-00046">This scatter plot shows foal birth weight and mare’s weight.</para>

        <media alt="A SCATTER PLOT THAT SHOWS MARE WEIGHT IN KILOGRAMS ON THE X-AXIS AND FOAL WEIGHT IN KILOGRAMS ON THE Y-AXIS.">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_03_13_03.jpg"/>
        </media>


        <exercise id="df1c0214-99ea-424d-be82-48c61718f632" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00047">What does the slope mean of a linear model on this scatter plot?</para>
            <para id="para-00048">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00049">the increases in foal weight in kg for each kg increase in mare weight</para>
            <para id="para-00050">the increase in mare weight in kg for each kg increase in foal weight</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00051">Correct! The slope represents the increase in foal weight in kg for each kg
              increase in mare weight</para>
            <para id="para-00052">Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2 – 3.1.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00053">The correct answer is the slope represents the increase in foal weight in kg for
              each kg increase in mare weight.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_7f65c0a6-2f8f-48ea-9e26-b922072ad0e7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00054">Will the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of a linear model make sense in this problem?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00055">Correct! No. When the mare weight is zero, the linear model will be above 0 on the
              <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis.</para>
            <para id="para-00056">Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00057">The correct answer is: No. When the mare weight is zero, the linear model will be
              above 0 on the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_07a82a5e-01fb-47af-a9cb-8008398d1fd9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00058">What does the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>548</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>108</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> mean in this problem?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00059">Correct! A mare weighing 548 pounds has a foal weighing 108 pounds.</para>
            <para id="para-00060">Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2 – 3.1.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00061">The correct answer is that a mare weighing 548 pounds has a foal weighing 108
              pounds.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
